---
// COOKBOOK — performance tuning (docs-refactor Phase 5, #1000; plan §8: dprCap / qualityTier / density).
//
// /docs/performance covers the SCHEDULER and what runs each frame. This page is the tuning layer:
// the three dials that actually move the cost, measured rather than asserted — including the
// `130 × density` rule that explains the contained-field surprise, and the honest note that
// setQualityTier has no read-back on JS (a fact read out of the parity matrix, not hand-typed).
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SeeAlso from '../../../components/SeeAlso.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../../components/StatusChip.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import PlatformSupport from '../../../components/PlatformSupport.astro';
import CookbookExample from '../../../components/CookbookExample.astro';

import tuningSrc from '../../../lib/cookbook/tuning.ts?raw';
import { runTuning } from '../../../lib/cookbook/tuning.ts';

const tuning = runTuning();

const governorJs = `import { QualityGovernor } from '@fundamental-engine/dom';

// The governor DETECTS sustained overruns; you decide what to do about them.
// <field-root> wires this for you. On a raw createField you own the loop:
const gov = new QualityGovernor(16.67);

function frame(t) {
  const tier = gov.feed(t);          // 0 full · 1 effects reduced · 2 minimal · 3 paused
  if (tier !== null) field.setQualityTier(tier);
  requestAnimationFrame(frame);
}
requestAnimationFrame(frame);`;

const dprJs = `// The backing store is the dominant fill cost on a retina display.
// Effective DPR is min(devicePixelRatio, dprCap).
const field = createField(canvas, { host, dprCap: 1.5 });

field.setDprCap(1); // and it is runtime-settable`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Fundamental — Performance tuning"
  description="The three dials that move the cost of a field: density (the pool is 130 x density, field-wide), dprCap (the backing-store ceiling), and qualityTier (the degradation ladder) — with inspectBudget to judge a configuration before it ships."
>
  <header class="docs-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Cookbook</p>
    <h1>Performance <span class="accent">tuning</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Three dials carry almost all of the cost: how much matter there is, how many device pixels it
      is drawn into, and how much the engine is allowed to give up when a device cannot keep pace.
      This page measures each one rather than asserting it.
    </p>
    <StatusChip label="Shipped · measured at build time" tone="stable" />
  </header>

  <div class="callout">
    <b>Different page, different question.</b>
    <a href="/docs/performance">/docs/performance</a> explains the six-phase scheduler and what runs
    each frame — the <em>architecture</em> of the cost. This page is the <em>tuning</em>: which knob
    to turn, and what it actually does.
  </div>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="density">density — the pool is <code>130 × density</code>, field-wide</h2>
    <p>
      <code>density</code> is a particle-count multiplier over a base of 130. The number to
      internalise is that this is a <strong>field-wide count, not a per-area one</strong>: the pool
      does not grow because your field is large, and does not shrink because it is small.
    </p>
    <p>
      That single fact explains the most common contained-field complaint. A card-sized field still
      holds 130 particles spread across the whole volume, so each body gathers less and
      <code>--d</code> reads low. The fix is to raise <code>density</code> for a small field, not
      lower it — see <a href="/docs/cookbook/contained-fields">contained fields</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-options" symbol="density" />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="dpr">dprCap — the backing-store ceiling</h2>
    <p>
      The effective device-pixel ratio is <code>min(devicePixelRatio, dprCap)</code>. On a 3× display
      an uncapped field fills nine times the pixels of a 1× one for the same CSS box, which is why
      the shipped budget caps it at 2. Lower it before you start cutting matter: it costs
      sharpness, not behaviour.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'TypeScript', lang: 'ts', code: dprJs }]} />
    <p>
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-options" symbol="dprCap" />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="quality-tier">qualityTier — the degradation ladder</h2>
    <p>
      A tier is a coarse, reversible statement of how much the engine may give up: <b>0</b> full,
      <b>1</b> effects reduced, <b>2</b> minimal, <b>3</b> paused. At tier 2 and above the engine
      caps the effective DPR and drops the heatmap on its own levers. The
      <code>QualityGovernor</code> detects sustained overruns and tells you which tier to ask for;
      <code>&lt;field-root&gt;</code> wires that loop for you, and a raw <code>createField</code>
      does not.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'TypeScript', lang: 'ts', code: governorJs }]} />
    <div class="callout">
      <b>A cross-platform asymmetry worth knowing.</b> On JS, <code>setQualityTier</code> is
      <strong>write-only</strong> — the handle exposes no property to read the tier back, as the
      example below measures. The Swift and Kotlin handles both publish one. The support row is
      generated from the parity matrix, so it cannot drift from the ports:
      <PlatformSupport
        dimension="field-handle-properties"
        symbol="qualityTier"
        note="the read-back property — not on JS"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="budget">Judging a configuration before it ships</h2>
    <p>
      <code>inspectBudget(counts)</code> compares a configuration against the shipped budget and
      returns one finding per breach — no field required, so it runs in a unit test or a build step.
    </p>
    <CookbookExample
      title="The dials, measured"
      path="apps/site/src/lib/cookbook/tuning.ts"
      source={tuningSrc}
      result={tuning}
      caption="130 particles at density 1 and 33 at 0.25 — the rule, measured on a live pool rather than quoted. The findings array is a real over-budget verdict, and the tier read-back is false on JS."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="order">The order to turn them</h2>
    <ul class="model">
      <li><b>Measure first.</b> <code>createFieldPerf</code> gives you fps, frame budget, percentiles and dropped frames from your own rAF timestamps.</li>
      <li><b>Cap the pixels.</b> <code>dprCap</code> costs sharpness only.</li>
      <li><b>Then cut matter.</b> <code>density</code> changes how the field feels; do it deliberately.</li>
      <li><b>Let the governor handle the bad device.</b> A tier is reversible; a hard cutoff is not.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

